# Cookie banner

<h2 id="purpose">Purpose</h2>

A cookie banner tells people what a site stores in their browser and why. It offers a choice only where something is optional, such as usage measurement. Essential storage, like keeping you signed in, needs an explanation but no choice.

First establish what the site really stores. A banner cannot make an inaccurate description acceptable, and a local dashboard that stores nothing optional does not need one.

Home Assistant has no cookie banner, and the Civica theme does not manage cookie consent, sign-in or telemetry. This is a Civica pattern for a public page, such as a documentation site or a shared status page.

This page covers:

- Cookie banner
  - Civica cookie banner pattern

<h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>

- A region with a heading, such as “Cookies on Civica”.
- A short statement of what is stored and why, in plain words.
- A button to hide the notice, or accept and reject buttons where something is optional.
- A link to the full cookies page.
- After a choice, a short confirmation, and focus moved to the main content.

