<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

<h3 id="child-civica-cookie-banner-pattern"><code>Civica cookie banner pattern</code></h3>

Two forms:

- **Essential only.** One statement, a Hide this notice button and a link to the cookies page. Hiding it acknowledges the notice and does not give consent, because nothing optional is used.
- **Optional purpose.** A statement of each optional purpose, and Accept and Reject buttons of equal weight. Nothing is preselected and hiding the banner is never treated as consent.

How this site’s notice works:

- Selecting **Hide this notice** saves one cookie, `civica_docs_notice`, with the value `v1`.
- It lasts up to one year, applies only to this site’s path, uses `SameSite=Lax`, and is marked `Secure` on HTTPS.
- It holds no account or household information. Clearing it shows the notice again, and a new version of the notice asks again.
- After hiding, keyboard focus moves to the main content.
- If the browser blocks storage, the notice hides for this page and says it may appear again.
- With scripts off, the notice stays readable and the Hide button stays hidden. The footer links and the [Cookies page](../community/cookies.html) are always available.

The pattern has no Home Assistant theme variables. The text takes the page’s colours.

If you need something similar inside Home Assistant, use a Markdown card on a separate public page with a link to your privacy information. Home Assistant has no consent mechanism.

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- It makes storage visible and gives people a route to the full explanation.
- It cannot enforce a choice. Your site must not load optional code until someone accepts.
- It is not legal advice. Check what your own service must say.
- It is only as honest as its statement. Keep it in step with what the site stores.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

- State the purpose of each optional category in plain language.
- Give Accept and Reject equal prominence.
- Do not preselect a checkbox or treat dismissal as consent.
- Keep the banner in the flow of the page, so it does not cover focused content.
- Honour a rejection before loading optional code, and give a lasting route back to change the choice.
- Confirm a choice only after it is saved. If saving fails, keep the choice available and explain.
- Say how long a choice lasts, using facts from your own service.

Success criteria that apply:

- 1.3.1 Info and Relationships and 2.4.3 Focus Order: a labelled region in a sensible reading order.
- 2.1.1 Keyboard and 2.4.7 Focus Visible: every button is reachable with a visible focus.
- 2.4.11 Focus Not Obscured (Minimum): the banner is in the flow, not an overlay.
- 1.4.10 Reflow: it works at 320 CSS pixels.
- 3.2.1 On Focus and 3.2.2 On Input: nothing happens until a button is used.
- 4.1.3 Status Messages: the confirmation is a status message.

Test reject, accept, coming back later, changing a choice, blocked storage and scripts off. Check what is really stored and sent in your browser’s tools; a picture cannot show it.

